ÿØÿà␀JFIF␀,,␀␀ÿþ²Radio telescope at the National Radio Astronomy Observatory in Green Bank, West Virginia.  A radio telescope has several main parts: a dish and antenna, a receiver, a detector, and an analyzer. It collects radio signals from space and focuses them on the antenna. A larger dish, such as that on this 100-meter-diameter Robert C. Byrd radio telescope, will collect voluminous radio waves and lead to a stronger signal at the antenna.ÿítPhotoshop 3.0␀8BIMí␀␀␀␀␀,␀␀␀␀,␀␀␀␀8BIMó␀␀␀␀␀	␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀8BIMõ␀␀␀␀␀H␀/ff␀␀lff␀␀␀␀␀␀␀/ff␀␀¡™š␀␀␀␀␀␀␀2␀␀␀␀Z␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀5␀␀␀␀-␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀8BIMø␀␀␀␀␀p␀␀ÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿè␀␀␀␀ÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿè␀␀␀␀ÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿè␀␀␀␀ÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿÿè␀␀8BIM␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀@␀␀@␀␀␀␀8BIM␀␀␀␀“␀␀␀␀␀␀ ␀␀␀k␀␀à␀␀È ␀␀w␀␀ÿØÿí␀Adobe_CM␀ÿî␀Adobe␀d€␀␀␀ÿÛ␀„␀			
