   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Radio telescope at the National Radio Astronomy Observatory in Green Bank, West Virginia.  A radio telescope has several main parts: a dish and antenna, a receiver, a detector, and an analyzer. It collects radio signals from space and focuses them on the antenna. A larger dish, such as that on this 100-meter-diameter Robert C. Byrd radio telescope, will collect voluminous radio waves and lead to a stronger signal at the antenna.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
